---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/japangaap/asbj24.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope — Three Categories of Change" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Changes in Accounting Policy — Retrospective Application" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Changes in Accounting Estimates — Prospective Application" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Correction of Prior Period Errors — Retrospective Restatement" },
        { id: "s6", label: "THE MAJOR REFORM — Moving Away From the Old Cumulative-Effect Approach" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Distinguishing a Change in Estimate From a Change in Policy or an Error" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example — Retrospective Application of a Policy Change" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Worked Example — Prospective Application of an Estimate Change" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Relationship to IAS/IFRS" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="hub">
      <div class="hub-box">
        <div class="hub-title">ASBJ Statement No. 24 — Accounting Standard for Accounting Changes and Error Corrections</div>
        <div class="hub-sub">Extended reference poster · Japan GAAP (J-GAAP) · 会計上の変更及び誤謬の訂正に関する会計基準 · A major shift toward retrospective restatement, converging with IAS 8 · JPY examples</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">A landmark shift from catch-up profit adjustments to genuine restatement</div>
        <div class="principle">ASBJ Statement No. 24 governs HOW an entity accounts for THREE distinct types of change: CHANGES in accounting POLICY, CHANGES in accounting ESTIMATES, and CORRECTIONS of PRIOR PERIOD errors — each requiring a DIFFERENT accounting treatment. This Statement represented a GENUINELY MAJOR reform of Japanese practice, moving decisively AWAY from an OLDER approach that recognised the CUMULATIVE effect of such changes ENTIRELY within CURRENT period profit or loss, TOWARD retrospective RESTATEMENT of prior periods — bringing J-GAAP much CLOSER to IAS 8 internationally (Section 6).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — Three Categories of Change</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Policy changes, estimate changes, and error corrections each treated differently</div>
        <div class="principle">This Statement applies WHENEVER an entity CHANGES an accounting POLICY, REVISES an accounting ESTIMATE, or DISCOVERS an ERROR in PREVIOUSLY issued financial statements — establishing DISTINCT treatment rules for EACH of these THREE genuinely different situations.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Changes in Accounting Policy — Retrospective Application</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Prior period comparatives are restated as if the new policy always applied</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Where an entity CHANGES an accounting POLICY (e.g. switching inventory cost formulas), the CHANGE is applied RETROSPECTIVELY — RESTATING PRIOR PERIOD comparative financial statements AS IF the NEW policy had ALWAYS been applied, UNLESS doing so is IMPRACTICABLE, in which case the change is applied PROSPECTIVELY from the EARLIEST practicable date instead.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Changes in Accounting Estimates — Prospective Application</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">No restatement — the revised estimate applies only going forward</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Where an entity REVISES an accounting ESTIMATE (e.g. a revised useful life for depreciation, or an updated allowance for doubtful accounts), the CHANGE is applied PROSPECTIVELY — affecting ONLY the CURRENT and FUTURE periods, with NO restatement of PRIOR periods — reflecting that estimate REVISIONS represent NEW, updated information becoming available over time, rather than a correction of something that was WRONG when originally determined.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Correction of Prior Period Errors — Retrospective Restatement</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Prior period financial statements are corrected as if the error never occurred</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Where a MATERIAL Error in PREVIOUSLY issued financial statements is DISCOVERED (e.g. a mathematical mistake, a misapplication of an accounting policy, or fraud), the correction is applied RETROSPECTIVELY — RESTATING the AFFECTED prior period financial statements AS IF the error had NEVER occurred, ensuring COMPARATIVE information presented is genuinely ACCURATE.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>THE MAJOR REFORM — Moving Away From the Old Cumulative-Effect Approach</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">A fundamental shift in how Japanese practice treats the past</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">PRIOR to this Statement, JAPANESE practice HISTORICALLY recognised the CUMULATIVE Effect of an accounting policy CHANGE or ERROR correction ENTIRELY within CURRENT period profit or loss — a "CATCH-UP" adjustment lumping the ENTIRE historical impact into the year the CHANGE was made or the error DISCOVERED, WITHOUT restating prior periods' OWN reported figures. ASBJ Statement No. 24 represented a GENUINELY MAJOR reform, moving DECISIVELY toward RETROSPECTIVE restatement instead — a change with REAL practical consequences for how comparability across periods is achieved, and bringing J-GAAP MUCH CLOSER to IAS 8's internationally established approach.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Distinguishing a Change in Estimate From a Change in Policy or an Error</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Genuine judgement is often required</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">DISTINGUISHING a genuine CHANGE in ESTIMATE (prospective) from a CHANGE in POLICY or an ERROR correction (both retrospective) can require SIGNIFICANT judgement — for example, a REVISED depreciation method MIGHT reflect a genuine change in POLICY, OR it might reflect a change in the ESTIMATED PATTERN of economic benefit consumption (an estimate change) — the CORRECT classification determines WHETHER prior periods are restated at all.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Retrospective Application of a Policy Change</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Restating the prior year comparative</div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: Switching inventory cost formulas</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A company switches its inventory costing method from weighted average to FIFO, effective for the current year. Under the NEW FIFO method, PRIOR year inventory and cost of sales would have been ¥15,000,000 lower.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">The company RESTATES the PRIOR year's comparative financial statements to reflect the ¥15,000,000 adjustment AS IF FIFO had ALWAYS been applied — presenting BOTH the current and prior year using a CONSISTENT, comparable basis, rather than recognising the ENTIRE ¥15,000,000 as a single CATCH-UP adjustment in the CURRENT year (as OLDER Japanese practice would have done).</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Prospective Application of an Estimate Change</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">No restatement, only a forward-looking change</div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: Revising an asset's useful life</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A company originally estimated a machine's useful life at 10 years; after 4 years, it revises the estimate to 6 REMAINING years (a total of 10 years, unchanged from the ORIGINAL estimate but reflecting updated information), based on new information about wear patterns.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">The company applies the REVISED estimate PROSPECTIVELY — spreading the machine's REMAINING carrying amount over the NEW 6-year remaining life GOING FORWARD, WITHOUT restating the PRIOR 4 years' depreciation figures, since this represents a genuine CHANGE IN ESTIMATE, not a policy change or error correction.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">What ASBJ Statement No. 24 requires</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Nature and reason for an accounting policy change, estimate change, or error correction</span>
          <span>Amount of the adjustment for the current period and, where applicable, each prior period presented</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Correctly classifying a change as a policy change, estimate change, or error correction</span>
              <span>Whether retrospective restatement is impracticable in specific circumstances</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Quantifying the retrospective adjustment for prior periods</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — J-GAAP vs IAS 8</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-12-12">
            <span>Feature</span>
            <span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">J-GAAP (ASBJ Statement No. 24)</span>
            <span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 8)</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-12-12"><span>Policy changes</span><span>Retrospective restatement</span><span>Retrospective restatement — converged</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-12-12"><span>Estimate changes</span><span>Prospective application</span><span>Prospective application — converged</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-12-12"><span>Error corrections</span><span>Retrospective restatement</span><span>Retrospective restatement — converged</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">This Statement represents ONE of the CLEAREST examples of a DELIBERATE, comprehensive J-GAAP reform undertaken SPECIFICALLY to align with an existing IFRS standard — the RESULTING framework is now GENUINELY, THOROUGHLY converged with IAS 8.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Where every accounting change or error correction lands</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-16-1-14">
            <span>Event</span>
            <span>Statement(s) affected</span>
            <span>Notes</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Policy change / error correction</span><span>All statements, prior periods restated</span><span>Retrospective application</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Estimate change</span><span>Current and future periods only</span><span>Prospective application, no restatement</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-08-2">
            <span>Standard</span>
            <span>Connection</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 9</span><span>Measurement of Inventories — a common context in which policy changes (e.g. cost formula changes) arise</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Retrospective application</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">Restating prior period comparative financial statements as if a new accounting policy had always applied, or as if a prior period error never occurred.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last">
          <div class="glossary-term">Prospective application</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">Applying a revised accounting estimate only to the current and future periods, without restating prior periods.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="legend">
      <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Japan GAAP terminology)</div>
      <div class="lrow">
        <span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet (貸借対照表)
        <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> Statement of Income / Profit and Loss Statement (損益計算書)
        <span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span> 純資産の部
        <span class="badge be">SCNA</span> Statement of Changes in Net Assets (株主資本等変動計算書)
        <span class="badge bc">SCF</span> Statement of Cash Flows (キャッシュ・フロー計算書)
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="footer-note">ASBJ Statement No. 24 — Accounting Standard for Accounting Changes and Error Corrections · Extended Reference Poster · Self-contained, no internet connection required</div>
  </div>

</StandardPageLayout>
